From: Manuel Huber <user5@example.test>
The IPE policy keyring is allocated unlinked, so userspace would otherwise have to scrape /proc/keys to name it.
Signed-off-by: Manuel Huber <user5@example.test> --- Documentation/security/keys/core.rst | 9 +++++++++ include/linux/key.h | 2 ++ include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h | 1 + security/ipe/keyring.c | 1 + security/keys/process_keys.c | 23 +++++++++++++++++++++++ 5 files changed, 36 insertions(+)
diff --git a/Documentation/security/keys/core.rst b/Documentation/security/keys/core.rst index 326b8a973828..e4ecda5c2525 100644 --- a/Documentation/security/keys/core.rst +++ b/Documentation/security/keys/core.rst @@ -373,6 +373,15 @@ process making the call:: KEY_SPEC_REQKEY_AUTH_KEY -7 assumed request_key() authorisation key
+There are also values that refer to a specific kernel keyring rather than to +anything belonging to the caller. These keyrings are not linked into any +keyring, so a search cannot reach them and the ID is the only way to name +one:: + + CONSTANT VALUE KEY REFERENCED + ============================== ====== =========================== + KEY_SPEC_IPE_KEYRING -12 the ".ipe" keyring +
The main syscalls are:
diff --git a/include/linux/key.h b/include/linux/key.h index 81b8f05c6898..9c2ac0c70d56 100644 --- a/include/linux/key.h +++ b/include/linux/key.h @@ -440,6 +440,8 @@ extern key_ref_t keyring_search(key_ref_t keyring, extern int keyring_restrict(key_ref_t keyring, const char *type, const char *restriction);
+extern void key_register_ipe_keyring(struct key *keyring); + extern struct key *key_lookup(key_serial_t id);
static inline key_serial_t key_serial(const struct key *key) diff --git a/include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h b/include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h index 4c8884eea808..e42fca286b15 100644 --- a/include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h +++ b/include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h @@ -24,6 +24,7 @@ #define KEY_SPEC_GROUP_KEYRING -6 /* - key ID for GID-specific keyring */ #define KEY_SPEC_REQKEY_AUTH_KEY -7 /* - key ID for assumed request_key auth key */ #define KEY_SPEC_REQUESTOR_KEYRING -8 /* - key ID for request_key() dest keyring */ +#define KEY_SPEC_IPE_KEYRING -12 /* - key ID for the .ipe keyring */
/* request-key default keyrings */ #define KEY_REQKEY_DEFL_NO_CHANGE -1 diff --git a/security/ipe/keyring.c b/security/ipe/keyring.c index ea8d164b62a6..5158dc7310a7 100644 --- a/security/ipe/keyring.c +++ b/security/ipe/keyring.c @@ -74,6 +74,7 @@ int __init ipe_keyring_init(void) }
ipe_keyring = keyring; + key_register_ipe_keyring(ipe_keyring);
return 0; } diff --git a/security/keys/process_keys.c b/security/keys/process_keys.c index a63c46bb2d14..20d6faefb75e 100644 --- a/security/keys/process_keys.c +++ b/security/keys/process_keys.c @@ -22,6 +22,9 @@ /* Session keyring create vs join semaphore */ static DEFINE_MUTEX(key_session_mutex);
+/* IPE keyring reachable through KEY_SPEC_IPE_KEYRING */ +static struct key *ipe_keyring __ro_after_init; + /* The root user's tracking struct */ struct key_user root_key_user = { .usage = REFCOUNT_INIT(3), @@ -590,6 +593,18 @@ bool lookup_user_key_possessed(const struct key *key, return key == match_data->raw_data; }
+/** + * key_register_ipe_keyring - Publish the IPE keyring + * @keyring: The keyring to publish + * + * Make @keyring reachable by userspace through the KEY_SPEC_IPE_KEYRING + * special key ID. Called once, from an initcall, and never undone. + */ +void key_register_ipe_keyring(struct key *keyring) +{ + ipe_keyring = keyring; +} + /* * Look up a key ID given us by userspace with a given permissions mask to get * the key it refers to. @@ -741,6 +756,14 @@ key_ref_t lookup_user_key(key_serial_t id, unsigned long lflags, key_ref = make_key_ref(key, 1); break;
+ case KEY_SPEC_IPE_KEYRING: + key = ipe_keyring; + if (!key) + goto error; + __key_get(key); + key_ref = make_key_ref(key, 0); + break; + default: key_ref = ERR_PTR(-EINVAL); if (id < 1)
-- 2.53.0

From: Manuel Huber <user5@example.test>
The BPF, dm-verity, and fs-verity keyrings have well-known IDs which allow userspace to directly address them. This commit follows the same pattern for the .ipe keyring.
This provides a direct interface independent of resolving %:.ipe through process keyrings and /proc/keys.
Signed-off-by: Manuel Huber <user5@example.test> --- Documentation/security/keys/core.rst | 12 ++++++++++++ include/linux/key.h | 2 ++ include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h | 1 + security/ipe/keyring.c | 2 ++ security/keys/process_keys.c | 23 +++++++++++++++++++++++ 5 files changed, 40 insertions(+)
diff --git a/Documentation/security/keys/core.rst b/Documentation/security/keys/core.rst index 326b8a973828..64b37817a335 100644 --- a/Documentation/security/keys/core.rst +++ b/Documentation/security/keys/core.rst @@ -373,6 +373,18 @@ process making the call:: KEY_SPEC_REQKEY_AUTH_KEY -7 assumed request_key() authorisation key
+There are also values that refer to a specific kernel keyring rather than to +anything belonging to the caller. These well-known IDs allow userspace to +name kernel keyrings directly even when they are not linked into a process +keyring:: + + CONSTANT VALUE KEY REFERENCED + ============================== ====== =========================== + KEY_SPEC_IPE_KEYRING -12 the ".ipe" keyring + +The availability of a subsystem keyring depends on its kernel configuration. +Looking up an unavailable keyring returns ``ENOKEY``. +
The main syscalls are:
diff --git a/include/linux/key.h b/include/linux/key.h index 81b8f05c6898..9c2ac0c70d56 100644 --- a/include/linux/key.h +++ b/include/linux/key.h @@ -440,6 +440,8 @@ extern key_ref_t keyring_search(key_ref_t keyring, extern int keyring_restrict(key_ref_t keyring, const char *type, const char *restriction);
+extern void key_register_ipe_keyring(struct key *keyring); + extern struct key *key_lookup(key_serial_t id);
static inline key_serial_t key_serial(const struct key *key) diff --git a/include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h b/include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h index 4c8884eea808..e42fca286b15 100644 --- a/include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h +++ b/include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h @@ -24,6 +24,7 @@ #define KEY_SPEC_GROUP_KEYRING -6 /* - key ID for GID-specific keyring */ #define KEY_SPEC_REQKEY_AUTH_KEY -7 /* - key ID for assumed request_key auth key */ #define KEY_SPEC_REQUESTOR_KEYRING -8 /* - key ID for request_key() dest keyring */ +#define KEY_SPEC_IPE_KEYRING -12 /* - key ID for the .ipe keyring */
/* request-key default keyrings */ #define KEY_REQKEY_DEFL_NO_CHANGE -1 diff --git a/security/ipe/keyring.c b/security/ipe/keyring.c index 8502f825945d..14626df46e71 100644 --- a/security/ipe/keyring.c +++ b/security/ipe/keyring.c @@ -26,5 +26,7 @@ int __init ipe_keyring_init(void) KEY_ALLOC_NOT_IN_QUOTA, NULL, NULL); if (IS_ERR(ipe_keyring)) panic("failed to allocate \".ipe\" keyring"); + key_register_ipe_keyring(ipe_keyring); + return 0; } diff --git a/security/keys/process_keys.c b/security/keys/process_keys.c index a63c46bb2d14..20d6faefb75e 100644 --- a/security/keys/process_keys.c +++ b/security/keys/process_keys.c @@ -22,6 +22,9 @@ /* Session keyring create vs join semaphore */ static DEFINE_MUTEX(key_session_mutex);
+/* IPE keyring reachable through KEY_SPEC_IPE_KEYRING */ +static struct key *ipe_keyring __ro_after_init; + /* The root user's tracking struct */ struct key_user root_key_user = { .usage = REFCOUNT_INIT(3), @@ -590,6 +593,18 @@ bool lookup_user_key_possessed(const struct key *key, return key == match_data->raw_data; }
+/** + * key_register_ipe_keyring - Publish the IPE keyring + * @keyring: The keyring to publish + * + * Make @keyring reachable by userspace through the KEY_SPEC_IPE_KEYRING + * special key ID. Called once, from an initcall, and never undone. + */ +void key_register_ipe_keyring(struct key *keyring) +{ + ipe_keyring = keyring; +} + /* * Look up a key ID given us by userspace with a given permissions mask to get * the key it refers to. @@ -741,6 +756,14 @@ key_ref_t lookup_user_key(key_serial_t id, unsigned long lflags, key_ref = make_key_ref(key, 1); break;
+ case KEY_SPEC_IPE_KEYRING: + key = ipe_keyring; + if (!key) + goto error; + __key_get(key); + key_ref = make_key_ref(key, 0); + break; + default: key_ref = ERR_PTR(-EINVAL); if (id < 1)
-- 2.53.0

Thank you for your contribution! Sashiko AI review found 1 potential issue(s) to consider:
- [Low] keys: build failure in bpf_lookup_keyring() after removing struct key.keys
--

commit 65b3c94e1ad80dd7e23ce523a687b926fdca188c
Author: Jarkko Sakkinen <user17@example.test>

KEYS: Replace assoc_array with rhashtable

This commit replaces the associative array implementation in the keyring
subsystem with rhashtable and an RCU-protected list using keyring_index and
keyring_link structures.

> diff --git a/include/linux/key.h b/include/linux/key.h
> index 20ca43f5eab1f..597131e150186 100644
> --- a/include/linux/key.h
> +++ b/include/linux/key.h

[ ... ]

> @@ -271,7 +260,7 @@ struct key {
>  		struct {
>  			/* Keyring bits */
>  			struct list_head name_link;
> -			struct assoc_array keys;
> +			struct keyring_index __rcu *index;
>  		};
>  	};

[Severity: Low]
Does removing the keys member from struct key break the build in
kernel/bpf/keys.c when both keys and bpf syscall support are enabled?

While commit 99fefa08474b ("KEYS: Introduce keyring_is_empty()") converted
dm-verity and fs-verity to use keyring_is_empty(), bpf_lookup_keyring() in
kernel/bpf/keys.c still accesses bpf_keyring->keys.nr_leaves_on_tree:

kernel/bpf/keys.c:bpf_lookup_keyring() {
	struct bpf_key *bkey;

	if (!bpf_keyring)
		return NULL;
	if (!READ_ONCE(bpf_keyring->keys.nr_leaves_on_tree) ||
	    !READ_ONCE(bpf_keyring->restrict_link))
		return NULL;
	...
}

Should bpf_lookup_keyring() be updated to call keyring_is_empty(bpf_keyring)
instead?

-- 
Sashiko AI review · https://sashiko.dev/#/patchset/user492@example.test?part=5

security_inode_init_security() returns early when no LSM reserves an xattr slot, so the inode_init_security hooks never run on a kernel whose LSMs provide no xattr. An LSM can then not use the hook to observe inode creation unless some other LSM happens to label inodes.
Call the hooks regardless of the reserved xattr count, and only skip allocating the xattr array when no slot is reserved. LSMs that write xattrs still need a reserved slot, so they are unaffected. Landlock uses the hook in the next patch to track the files a sandboxed task creates.
Assisted-by: LLM Signed-off-by: Farid Zakaria <user656@example.test> --- security/security.c | 9 +++++---- 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)
diff --git a/security/security.c b/security/security.c index b284e24e3771..bb94c94d97a5 100644 --- a/security/security.c +++ b/security/security.c @@ -1353,10 +1353,11 @@ int security_inode_init_security(struct inode *inode, struct inode *dir, if (unlikely(IS_PRIVATE(inode))) return 0;
- if (!blob_sizes.lbs_xattr_count) - return 0; - - if (initxattrs) { + /* + * Even without any xattr to provide, LSMs (e.g. Landlock) may need to + * track inode creations. + */ + if (initxattrs && blob_sizes.lbs_xattr_count) { /* Allocate +1 as terminator. */ new_xattrs = kcalloc(blob_sizes.lbs_xattr_count + 1, sizeof(*new_xattrs), GFP_NOFS);
-- 2.55.0

I'll just run through three issues that I forgot to update for this patch set version. I think it was good because probably these could use some feedback.
On Fri, Oct 23, 2026 at 03:09:25PM +0300, Jarkko Sakkinen wrote: > + WARN_ON(rhashtable_remove_fast(&links->ht, &link->node, > + keyring_link_params)); > + list_del_rcu(&link->list); > + WRITE_ONCE(links->nr, links->nr - 1); > + call_rcu(&link->rcu, keyring_link_free_rcu);
keyring_remove_link() is called by __key_unlink() and keyring_gc(), which are infallible operations.
At minimum this should be changed as:
if (WARN_ON(rhashtable_remove_fast(/* ... */)) return;
/* ... */
Alternatively, keyring_remove_link() could be fallible operation, and leave that decision up to the callers.
> + WARN_ON(rhashtable_replace_fast(&links->ht, &old->node, > + &link->node, > + keyring_link_params)); > + list_replace_rcu(&old->list, &link->list); > + call_rcu(&old->rcu, keyring_link_free_rcu);
__key_link() is called by __key_instantiate_and_link(), key_link(), key_move(), construct_alloc_key() and key_reject_and_link() i.e., by majority fallible functions.
Are all of these call sites such that if __key_link() was a fallible operation it would be impossible roll back, or is this something that could be addressed better?
> + WARN_ON(rhashtable_remove_fast(&keyring_index_locked(keyring)->ht, > + &link->node, > + keyring_link_params)); > + call_rcu(&link->rcu, keyring_link_free_rcu);
__key_link_end() goes along the lines of keyring_link_remove().
However these are decided I will remove keyring_link_remove(). There's enough symbols to get confused already, it has only two call sites and frankly I'd just want to see its contents open-coded in those sites. And it makes the level symmetric with __key_link_end().
keyring_link_remove() is type of slop that needs to be wiped away constantly from any LLM sourced code, at least in my experience as models tend to build on top of that design, and soon you have a nest.
Br, Jarkko

